const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  { q: "Are calorie calculators accurate?", a: "Calorie calculators provide a highly educated estimate, but they are not 100% perfectly accurate. Because metabolic rates differ by individual, a calculator using the Mifflin-St Jeor equation is usually accurate within a 10% to 15% margin of error." },
  { q: "Why is my calorie calculator wrong?", a: "If your calorie calculator seems wrong, it is usually because of an overestimated 'activity level' multiplier. People often overestimate how many calories they burn through exercise. Always select a lower activity tier (like 'Sedentary' or 'Lightly Active') for a more accurate baseline." },
  { q: "Which calorie equation is the most accurate?", a: "The Mifflin-St Jeor equation is widely considered by the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics to be the most accurate standard formula for calculating resting metabolic rate in healthy adults, outperforming older models like the Harris-Benedict equation." },
  { q: "Can a calorie calculator underestimate my metabolism?", a: "Yes. If you have a high amount of lean muscle mass, standard calorie calculators might underestimate your metabolism. Muscle burns more calories at rest than fat. In these cases, the Katch-McArdle formula (which factors in body fat percentage) is more accurate." },
  { q: "Are Apple Watch or Fitbit calorie trackers accurate?", a: "Studies show that wrist-based fitness trackers are notoriously inaccurate for tracking calories burned, often overestimating energy expenditure by 20% to 90%. An online BMR/TDEE calculator is generally a safer starting point for diet planning." }
];
